iginal prompt plus the proposed tokens in a single forward pass and computes its own probabilities for every position in that sequence. This lets the target model verify multiple candidate tokens at once instead of generating them one by one, which is the usual bottleneck in autoregressive decoding.\n\nThe key trick is a rejection test that preserves the target model\u2019s output distribution. For each proposed token, the method compares the draft probability with the target probability. If the target model is at least as confident as the draft, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. Because this correction is derived from the target model\u2019s probabilities, the final sequence has the same distribution as if the target model had decoded normally, even though some tokens were accepted in bulk.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, batch size, and hardware efficiency. A good draft model that matches the target\u2019s behavior can accept several tokens per target pass, reducing the number of expensive target forward passes. The two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are a low acceptance rate because the draft model is too small or poorly aligned with the target, and high overhead from running the draft model, memory transfers, or batched inference that makes the", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0022959820926189423], [760, -6.94197416305542]], [[1144, -1.2517053619376384e-05], [4087, -12.035672187805176]], [[4087, -0.17471657693386078], [310, -1.8388477563858032]], [[1156, -0.007099618203938007], [303, -4.9934983253479]], [[579, -0.2514456808567047], [25, -1.7843378782272339]], [[1622, -0.009867916814982891], [3296, -4.958880424499512]], [[25, -0.3183542788028717], [13, -1.3407560586929321]], [